Thanks again for the useful review done. Concerning your doubts:

1. We are aware of this and we agree with the referee. We will try carry out more experiments in the future;

2. The request of a comparison with other prior works with fair settings is reasonable and we will try to satisfy it in the future; as for the memory and compute usage, these are reported in the Supplementary Material. It is also worth noting that even if ConvShort has the same complexity of BN in terms of memory/operations, it does not introduce some other complications (e.g., the examples in minibatches are actually independent);

3. We did not try to replicate Brock et al. result; what we tried to do was to show that their initialization alone is not enough to train deep ResNets because their approach puts the network in an exploding gradients regime;

4. We will try our best, with our limited computational resources, to carry out experiments with more difficult cases (e.g., MiniImageNet, Transformers, larger batch sizes);

5. We will try to improve the rigorousness of the theoretical analysis.

Thanks again for the useful review done. Concerning your doubts:

1. Similar assumptions are often made, for instance He et al. (2015) make the same assumption of uncorrelation between gradients; We will better justify our assumption in a revised version of the paper;

2. The scaling technique from Brock et al. is different by ours, devised by a different rationale (we want to preserve backward signal, not forward) and it also requires additional tricks such as gradient clipping;

3. The gradient vanishing effect with our method is much less marked than the exploding gradients issue occurring with the other schemes (this can be better observed in Figure 4 in the supplementary material), and it is also a much less impactful numerical issue; moreover, vanishing in our setting is arguably due to minor perturbations, introduced, for example, by padding. The generality issue of our results will be solved by conducting experiments on harder problems.

We greatly thank the referee for the very detailed and, most of all, encouraging, understanding and constructive review of the referee. We only would like to remark that some results with ResNet-101 and ResNet-152 are reported in the supplementary material (still, we understand that these cases would probably not be considered deep enough models). Concerning your doubts:

1. The reviewer may be right; however, our intent lies in showing that high-quality training is possible without major modifications, rather than proposing a method that allows an increase in performance; 

2. A table with final performances will be added in a revised version of the manuscript;

3. As the reviewer correctly guessed, we have limited computational resources. Still, we will try to experiment with datasets like MiniImageNet and varying the batchsize;

4. Thanks, we will fix it as soon as possible.

Thanks again for the useful review done. Below you can find a reply for each of the issue you have highlight.

1. In the work from De et al. (2020), the residual branch is modified, whereas in our approach we scale both the residual block and the skip connection branches; this has a significant impact on the theoretical analysis. We hypothesize that this is the reason why the sqrt(0.5) downscaling by De et al. does not work properly;

2. Compared to De et al. work, we have explored a different way to remove the Batch Normalization: instead of initially force the network towards the identity function we have define a robust initiliazation strategy able to maintain the variance of the important signals;

3. We agree that different baselines could be considered. However, as we outline in our manuscript, our aim is showing that a proper initialization is sufficient to make deep networks trainable without additional tricks such as clipping; moreover, the second aim of our experiement is to show that backward signal preservation is much more important than that of forward signal;

4. We will take into account the correct suggestion of the referee in the future;

5. Thank you, we are fixing these inaccuracies.
